WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.240
The F.D.A. Is Unsafe and Ineffective Dying patients can't access drugs that already passed every safety test.

2
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.900
They also can't join the trials testing those drugs. They can, however, die.

3
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:24.020
Nobody blocks that. In nineteen sixty-two, your Congress decided that after proving a drug won't kill you, sick people should

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.080
wait another eight point two years before they're allowed to take it.

5
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:35.540
The drug passed safety testing. It won't kill you, won't melt your liver, won't make your hair migrate to your back.

6
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:43.220
But you can't have it, because a committee needs to spend eight point two years making sure it works well enough.

7
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.520
You're not allowed to volunteer for the trials that would answer that question faster, either.

8
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.690
You are, however, allowed to die. This was called "safety.".

9
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.600
On Wishonia, when we confirm something won't harm you, we let you try it.

10
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.970
On Earth, you lock it in a cabinet and form a committee. The committee meets quarterly.

11
00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:11.520
You decompose on a different schedule. A Note on Blame. When this chapter says "the F.D.A.

12
00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.260
does X," read it as "the nineteen sixty-two law requires the F.D.A.

13
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.440
to do X.". Congress wrote the rules. The F.D.A. follows them.

14
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:29.080
Blaming the F.D.A. is like blaming the gun instead of the person who loaded it, aimed it at patients, and pulled the trigger

15
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:37.780
on a sixty-two-year schedule. "But What About Safety?". This is the part where humans panic, so let me be very precise about

16
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:46.920
what this chapter is (and isn't) proposing: keep Phase One safety testing (it works, it caught thalidomide, nobody is suggesting

17
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:54.460
you skip it). Do *radically more* efficacy testing, on *real patients*, with *transparent results*.

18
00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.200
But stop holding known-safe drugs hostage while you run the trials.

19
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:07.680
On Wishonia, we have a word for "preventing someone from accessing a safe substance that could help them while they die."

20
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:16.580
The word is "murder." You call it "the approval process.". The forty-four point one times Inefficiency Tax.

21
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:25.660
Compare two real-world systems for testing drugs: The Oxford RECOVERY Trial tested COVID treatments on forty-eight thousand

22
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:32.460
patients for five hundred dollars, and found a life-saving treatment in three months, like a normal species would.

23
00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:40.600
The post-nineteen sixty-two system averages forty-one thousand dollars per trial and takes over a decade to approve a new

24
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:49.520
drug, like humans would. Your efficacy testing system costs forty-four point one times more and takes thirty-two point eight

25
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:58.020
times longer than necessary. That's not inefficiency. That's a system performing exactly as designed, if the design goal

26
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:04.800
was to kill people slowly enough that nobody notices. The eight point two years Efficacy Lag.

27
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:11.640
Here's where your lifespan goes to die: Phase One (Safety Testing) takes two point three years.

28
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:19.480
It tests if the drug harms people: death, organ damage, mutations, serious adverse reactions, all the ways a molecule can

29
00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:28.140
ruin your day. *This part works. Keep it.* Phase Two and Three (Efficacy Testing) takes eight point two years.

30
00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.480
The drug is now *known safe*. But patients can't have it until the committee is satisfied.

31
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:42.820
*This is where people die waiting for a drug that already passed every safety check your species invented.* What the nineteen

32
00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:50.880
sixty-two Efficacy Requirements Changed. Before nineteen sixty-two, one hundred and forty-four thousand physicians tested

33
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:59.020
treatments on real patients and published results. Life expectancy grew three point eight two years every decade for eighty

34
00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:03.620
straight years. Things were going well. Naturally, Congress intervened.

35
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.600
Phase One safety testing (added in nineteen thirty-eight) prevented all U.S.

36
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:16.079
thalidomide deaths while Europe had thousands. So in nineteen sixty-two, Congress looked at a system that was working and

37
00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:23.360
thought, "What if we made this much worse?" They added massive pre-approval efficacy requirements via the Kefauver Harris

38
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.100
Amendment, blocking patient access for years during testing.

39
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.420
The cost of developing a drug went from twenty-four point seven million dollars to two point six billion dollars, a one

40
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:41.440
hundred and five times increase. New drug approvals dropped by seventy percent.

41
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:49.760
Life expectancy gains slowed from three point eight two years per decade to one point five four, a sixty percent reduction.

42
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.040
Time to approval stretched from two to three years to ten point five years.

43
00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:01.560
The temporal break is exact. Every metric got worse the moment the law passed.

44
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:08.700
Like flipping a switch from "progress" to "paperwork." The one hundred and five times cost increase is rigorously documented.

45
00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:14.720
See the Drug Development Cost Analysis website for methodology, sensitivity analysis, and source data.

46
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:23.920
The Modern Consequences. Even if you think historical analysis is just opinions with footnotes, the current system's failures

47
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:30.520
are undeniable: A cure passes safety testing. You can't take it (pending efficacy review).

48
00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.700
You can't join the trial either (too sick, too old, wrong medications).

49
00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:41.820
You die. The F.D.A. approves the cure. Pharmaceutical companies charge your widow ten thousand dollars per pill.

50
00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.640
Beautiful system if you're a mortician or bankruptcy lawyer.

51
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:56.010
High Costs Kill Innovation, Reward Monopoly. Before nineteen sixty-two: Genius scientist invents cure, raises a few million,

52
00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:00.720
tests safety, gives it to people. Like a civilization that wants to survive would.

53
00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:06.580
Now: Genius scientist must convince one of three mega-corporations to fund trials.

54
00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:13.820
Those corporations already sell inferior drugs for the same condition, bringing in hundreds of millions a year per drug.

55
00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:22.280
The math for the mega-corporation: Option one: Develop the better drug Cost: two point six billion dollars on average to

56
00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:29.100
get a single drug to market. That figure already includes the cost of the nine candidates that fail for every one that succeeds,

57
00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:36.680
so the ten percent success rate is already priced in. Best case, the drug works: you now have a better drug for the same

58
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.690
condition. But your old drug, which was bringing in hundreds of millions a year, is now obsolete.

59
00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:47.210
You spent two point six billion dollars to replace your own revenue stream.

60
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.520
Every year the new drug is on the market, it's earning roughly what the old drug would have earned anyway, minus the two

61
00:06:53.520 --> 00:07:01.900
point six billion dollar hole you dug getting here. Option two: Buy the patent, put it on a shelf Your inferior drug keeps

62
00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:08.540
bringing in hundreds of millions a year. Total cost: whatever the patent costs, which is rounding error compared to two

63
00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:14.180
point six billion dollars. Which would you choose if you were a rational sociopath in a suit?

64
00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.040
The profit incentive doesn't just fail to reward better treatments.

65
00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:26.680
It actively punishes them. The best possible outcome of Option One is the same annual revenue you already had, minus two

66
00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:31.110
point six billion dollars. Curing disease is economically irrational.

67
00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.520
The system doesn't need a conspiracy to suppress cures. It just needs the math.

68
00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:45.520
Off-Patent Drugs and Rare Diseases: Mathematically Doomed. The nineteen sixty-two law made it mathematically impossible

69
00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:53.300
to cure diseases that aren't profitable enough: Ninety-five percent of diseases are rare: development cost of two point

70
00:07:53.300 --> 00:08:00.280
six billion dollars divided by a patient population of about ten thousand patients equals two hundred and sixty thousand

71
00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:08.000
dollars per patient. No patent equals no funding: off-patent drugs can't attract billion-dollar investments.

72
00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:14.860
Pre-specification kills serendipity: must predict what drug cures before testing.

73
00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:22.280
When something costs more, you get less of it. This is the first thing they teach in economics, and the last thing anyone

74
00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:27.800
considers when writing health policy. Ninety-five percent of diseases have zero treatments.

75
00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:32.659
Not because cures are impossible. Because cures are unprofitable.

76
00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:42.720
The Actual Death Toll of "Drug Lag". Economists call it "drug lag," which is a lovely, clinical way of saying "people rotting

77
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.540
to death in a queue for drugs that have already been confirmed not to hurt them.".

78
00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:53.720
Early estimates: twenty-one thousand to one hundred and twenty thousand American lives per decade.

79
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:59.620
But that was just the U.S. A comprehensive quantitative analysis using W.H.O.

80
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:08.360
mortality data estimates the *total* cost of the eight point two years *post-safety* efficacy delay (the lag *after* drugs

81
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:14.180
are known safe): four hundred and sixteen million eventually avoidable deaths.

82
00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:23.620
Including years lived with disability: seven point nine four billion disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s, of healthy

83
00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:31.360
human life deleted. Economic cost: one point one nine quadrillion dollars (twenty twenty-four U.S.

84
00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:38.600
dollars), reflecting those seven point nine four billion D.A.L.Y.s valued at one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per

85
00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:44.580
D.A.L.Y., the standard normative rate used in W.H.O. cost-effectiveness analyses.

86
00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:51.460
Even if those death estimates are off by a factor of ten, that's still tens of millions of deaths.

87
00:09:51.840 --> 00:10:00.020
And the inefficiency is independently verifiable: the efficacy testing process costs eighty-two times more per patient and

88
00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:05.860
takes thirty-two point eight times longer than proven alternatives like the Oxford RECOVERY trial.

89
00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:13.960
Those numbers don't depend on mortality estimates at all. Here's a news story from the Non-Existent Times by No One Ever,

90
00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:19.640
without a picture of all the people who die from lack of access to life-saving treatments that might have been.

91
00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.760
You can't photograph millions of people who died from *not* receiving a drug.

92
00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:29.680
There's no body with a toe tag reading "cause of death: regulatory delay."

93
00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:37.460
They die of cancer, heart disease, Alzheimer's. The delay that killed them is invisible, so nobody writes the headline.

94
00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:43.600
Types of Error in F.D.A. Approval Decision If the drug is beneficial and the F.D.A.

95
00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.420
allows the drug, that is a Correct Decision. If the drug is harmful and the F.D.A.

96
00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.980
allows the drug, victims are identifiable and might appear on Oprah.

97
00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:59.000
If the drug is beneficial and the F.D.A. does not allow the drug, victims are not identifiable or acknowledged.

98
00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:04.610
If the drug is harmful and the F.D.A. does not allow the drug, that is a Correct Decision.

99
00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:10.980
The most infamous case: beta-blockers. Europe used them to prevent heart attacks.

100
00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:16.920
The U.S. delayed for a decade, killing an estimated one hundred thousand Americans.

101
00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:22.560
More than died in Vietnam and Korea combined. From one drug delay.

102
00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:30.160
After nineteen sixty-two, U.S. life expectancy diverged from Switzerland's (which didn't introduce the same delays).

103
00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:36.360
More drug approvals in the eighties narrowed the gap. Fewer approvals in the nineties widened it again.

104
00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:44.460
How the Incentives Work. F.D.A. Regulator Decision Tree. Approve drug that later shows problems.

105
00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.560
Congressional hearing (televised). "F.D.A. APPROVED KILLER DRUG" headlines.

106
00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:54.670
Career ends. Pension threatened. Delay drug that could save lives.

107
00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:58.780
Nothing happens. Nobody writes a headline. Promotion on schedule.

108
00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:07.180
Retire to pharma job. This isn't conspiracy. It's just a system where the only career-ending move is letting patients access

109
00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.480
treatments. Approve a bad drug, your name is in the newspaper.

110
00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.330
Delay a good drug, the people who could have complained are dead.

111
00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.870
It's a perfect incentive structure if you're optimizing for cowardice.

112
00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:27.210
Dr. Henry I. Miller ran the F.D.A. team reviewing recombinant human insulin in the early nineteen eighties.

113
00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:32.920
Mountains of evidence showed it was safe and effective. His supervisor refused to approve it.

114
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.880
If someone died from the drug, heads would roll. If people died waiting for the drug?

115
00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.900
Nothing happens. Dead patients don't testify before Congress.

116
00:12:43.060 --> 00:13:03.580
The Math: Why Current Regulations Increase Total Harm. where: where: where: where: where: where: where: where: where: where:

117
00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:10.380
where: Even generously crediting ALL drug withdrawals to the nineteen sixty-two changes (ignoring that Phase one safety

118
00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:18.800
testing already existed), the prevented harm totals approximately two point five nine million disability-adjusted life years,

119
00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:24.980
or D.A.L.Y.s. The delay harm totals seven point nine four billion D.A.L.Y.s.

120
00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:33.220
For every one unit of harm prevented, the current system creates three thousand and seventy units of harm through delay.

121
00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:41.620
Clinical Trial Theater: Excluding eighty-six point one percent of Reality Makes Drugs More Dangerous.

122
00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:50.600
The F.D.A. requires rigorous trials to ensure safety. Then it tests the drugs on people who aren't sick, aren't old, aren't

123
00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:58.320
on other drugs, and aren't pregnant. Basically, it tests drugs on triathletes and then acts surprised when grandma has a

124
00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:07.060
reaction. Trials under the current system exclude: Patients over sixty-five include most people who actually take medications

125
00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:16.120
(excluded due to "comorbidities"). Patients under eighteen include all children (metabolism differs from adults).

126
00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:22.540
Pregnant women are excluded entirely (then drugs are prescribed during pregnancy anyway).

127
00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:30.060
Anyone with comorbidities includes the sickest patients most likely to have adverse reactions.

128
00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:37.180
Anyone on other medications includes everyone elderly (can't detect drug interactions).

129
00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:43.080
Anyone too far from trial sites includes poor and rural populations.

130
00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:52.240
This is like crash-testing a car by gently rolling it into a pillow, declaring it safe, and selling it to demolition derby

131
00:14:52.240 --> 00:15:01.900
drivers. Result: In antidepressant trials alone, eighty-six point one percent of real patients are excluded.

132
00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:10.720
The drugs are tested on the healthiest people alive, declared "safe," then handed to everyone the trial specifically avoided.

133
00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:20.540
This is how Vioxx, Fen-Phen, and Bextra all got approved, celebrated, prescribed to millions, and then withdrawn after killing

134
00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:24.380
people whose demographics the trials had carefully excluded.

135
00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:29.210
The eight point two years delay didn't make these drugs safer.

136
00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:35.020
It made them seem safer by hiding the danger behind an exclusion list.

137
00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:42.160
Testing drugs only on healthy volunteers to ensure safety is like testing parachutes only on the ground.

138
00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:48.980
No Long-Term Outcome Data. Data collection can be as short as several months.

139
00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:56.440
You take the drug for forty years. Pre-Specification Requirements Kill Innovation.

140
00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:04.800
The regulations require drug developers to predict exactly what a treatment will cure before testing it on humans.

141
00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:09.110
You must know the answer before you're allowed to ask the question.

142
00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:17.840
This is how science works on your planet, apparently. In two thousand and seven, Dendreon showed that its immunotherapy

143
00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:22.460
drug Provenge significantly reduced deaths from prostate cancer.

144
00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:28.320
The F.D.A. advisory committee agreed it worked. Then the F.D.A.

145
00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:36.700
rejected it anyway. Not because it didn't save lives. Because Dendreon filled out the wrong form about which lives it was

146
00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:40.880
planning to save. "People stopped dying" wasn't good enough.

147
00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:46.140
The paperwork predicting that people would stop dying wasn't filed in the right order.

148
00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:54.320
Three more years and another trial were required. During those three years, people who could have stopped dying continued

149
00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:02.420
dying, but at least the forms were correct. Due to these additional costs, Dendreon filed for chapter eleven bankruptcy.

150
00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:10.200
Killing two birds with one stone, as you say. The approval process killed the patients, then killed the company trying to

151
00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:17.480
save them. Although in reality far more birds were killed with that stone, but you can't count the people who would have

152
00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:22.900
been cured by a company you bankrupted. Small Trials Are Dangerous.

153
00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:27.220
Phase three trials test one thousand to three thousand patients.

154
00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:33.640
A one-in-ten-thousand adverse event is mathematically invisible in a trial of three thousand.

155
00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:43.960
Then the drug goes to millions. After approval, the F.D.A. monitors safety using F.A.E.R.S., a system where doctors voluntarily

156
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:49.980
report problems if they feel like it. It captures less than ten percent of actual adverse events.

157
00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:58.520
Vioxx killed an estimated thirty-eight thousand to fifty-five thousand Americans before this honor system noticed.

158
00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:06.520
That's more people than died in the entire Korean War, detected by a system that relies on doctors filling out optional

159
00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:16.220
paperwork. A decentralized F.D.A. with ten million participants catches one-in-ten thousand reactions, includes real populations,

160
00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:21.600
tracks long-term outcomes, and provides automated surveillance instead of voluntary paperwork.

161
00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:27.500
You have a fax machine and a suggestion box. One of these is a safety system.

162
00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:31.600
The other is a prayer with a eight point two years waiting period.

163
00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:40.800
The Negative Results Black Hole. Here's something that would be illegal if anybody important cared: Negative trial results

164
00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:47.200
published: thirty-seven percent. Positive trial results published: ninety-four percent.

165
00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:54.360
Money wasted repeating failed experiments: approximately one hundred billion dollars annually.

166
00:18:55.260 --> 00:19:01.860
Companies are allowed to hide when drugs don't work, which is like letting restaurants hide their health inspection failures.

167
00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:07.880
Actually, restaurants can't do that. Drugs get less transparency than a sandwich shop.

168
00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:12.880
Company: "We tested this drug!". Regulator: "Did it work?".

169
00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:21.620
Company: "...We tested this drug!". Pharmaceutical companies bury negative results deeper than Jimmy Hoffa.

170
00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:30.060
Other companies waste billions testing the same dead ends. Your insurance premiums fund this magnificent inefficiency.

171
00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:36.760
It's like casinos only having to report when people win. Countries That Don't Have Our "Safety".

172
00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:43.270
Japan gives conditional approval after Phase Two with real-world data collection.

173
00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:48.060
Time to patient: two to three years. Americans fly there for treatment.

174
00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:53.700
The E.U. lets terminal patients access experimental drugs at a doctor's discretion.

175
00:19:54.400 --> 00:20:01.220
F.D.A.: "But what if they die?" (They're already dying.). Right to Try in the U.S.

176
00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:06.800
passed despite F.D.A. opposition. The F.D.A. made it effectively impossible to use.

177
00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:13.150
Patients helped: less than two hundred total. Patients who wanted help: tens of thousands.

178
00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:22.100
On Wishonia, "Right to Try" is just called "rights." The fact that your species needed a special law to allow dying people

179
00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:29.920
to take known-safe drugs, and then made the law effectively unusable, is the most human thing I've ever observed.

180
00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:38.540
The COVID Test Fiasco. January twenty twenty: W.H.O. develops COVID test, world starts using it.

181
00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:45.420
February: F.D.A. blocks all non-C.D.C. tests to "ensure quality.".

182
00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:49.740
The C.D.C. tests were contaminated. Private labs begged to help.

183
00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:54.980
Late March: F.D.A. finally allows other tests after thousands die.

184
00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:03.000
The only approved tests didn't work. Ensuring quality control of broken tests is like T.S.A.

185
00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.460
confiscating water bottles while letting actual weapons through.

186
00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:11.500
Except people died. The Bottom Line. It is not a safety system.

187
00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:15.900
It is a filing system that produces corpses as a byproduct.

188
00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:24.540
The only thing missing is the political will to admit that a law signed before the Beatles existed might not be the pinnacle

189
00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:32.760
of pharmaceutical regulation. Technical Analysis. For the full quantitative analysis including methodology, sensitivity

